{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe `rebar` **MCP server**'s `review_plan` tool cannot run the LLM passes because the\nserver process's Python environment is missing the AWS Bedrock provider package. The\nreview falls back to the deterministic floor and returns an unsigned `INDETERMINATE`\nverdict, so **no plan-review attestation is minted** and the claim gate cannot be\nsatisfied through the MCP surface — even though the repo's own `.venv` has the provider\ninstalled and the local CLI can run the review.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Host: macOS, repo checkout `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar`.\n- MCP server: the configured `rebar` MCP server (tool `rebar-review_plan`).\n- Configured model/runner: `runner: pydantic_ai`, `model: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6`.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. From an MCP client, call `review_plan` on any reviewable ticket (observed on story\n   `6f74-2795-5ad4-4a56` / `unemployed-saddened-peccary`).\n2. Observe the returned verdict JSON.\n\n## Actual behaviour\n\n`review_plan` returns:\n\n```\n\"verdict\": \"INDETERMINATE\",\n\"coverage\": { \"llm_ran\": false, \"llm_unavailable\": true,\n  \"llm_error\": \"a bedrock model/provider is configured but the optional bedrock provider\n   package is not installed: pip install 'pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]'\" },\n\"signature\": { \"signed\": false, \"reason\": \"INDETERMINATE\" }\n```\n\nThe deterministic floor (P1–P11) runs and passes; only the LLM layer is unavailable. No\nsigned PASS attestation is produced.\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\nThe MCP server's `review_plan` runs the four-pass LLM review and (on a non-blocking PASS)\nsigns a plan-review attestation, matching the behaviour other sessions get and the local\nrepo-venv CLI (`.venv/bin/rebar review-plan`), whose interpreter has `pydantic_ai` with\nBedrock support available (verified: `importlib.util.find_spec('pydantic_ai.models.bedrock')`\nis truthy; `pydantic_ai 1.107.2`).\n\n## Root-cause hypothesis\n\nThe MCP server is launched from a Python environment (a global/stale install, not the repo\n`.venv`) that lacks the `[agents]` Bedrock extra, i.e. `pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]` is not\ninstalled for the server's interpreter. This is the exact failure mode\n`docs/local-dev-env.md` warns about: running a build that predates / omits the optional\nextras degrades `review-plan` / `verify-completion`. The MCP server must be started from a\nbuild with the `[agents]` extra (e.g. the repo `.venv` provisioned via `make install`, which\nruns `uv sync --locked --extra dev`).\n\n## Impact\n\nPlan-review (a certified claim gate op) cannot be completed through the MCP surface on this\nbox, so the documented \"route tracker ops through the MCP server\" path yields no attestation;\nwork must fall back to the local repo-venv CLI to review and sign.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The MCP server's launch environment has the Bedrock provider available: running\n  `python -c \"import importlib.util as u; assert u.find_spec('pydantic_ai.models.bedrock')\"`\n  against the server's interpreter exits 0 (checked on the box that hosts the MCP server).\n- [ ] `review_plan` via the MCP server on a reviewable ticket returns a verdict with\n  `coverage.llm_ran = true` (no `llm_unavailable`) and, on a non-blocking PASS, a signed\n  attestation (`signature.signed = true`) — checked by re-running the tool and inspecting the\n  returned JSON.\n- [ ] The fix/runbook records how the MCP server's interpreter is pinned to a build carrying\n  the `[agents]` extra (e.g. launched from the repo `.venv` per `docs/local-dev-env.md`), so\n  the regression cannot silently recur.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Not a plan defect and not a code defect in `src/rebar` — an MCP-server **deployment/env**\n  provisioning gap. The deterministic floor and the local CLI review path both work.\n- Evidence captured from a live `review_plan` call on 2026-08-27.\n\n## Fix plan (added after the root cause was PROVEN — see the RCA comment)\n\nThe reported hypothesis (a stale/global interpreter) is disproved. The server runs from the\ncompose-built container, whose dependency set is exactly what `Dockerfile.mcp` specifies —\nand that specification omits the configured provider. The fix therefore lands in the image\nand deployment definition, not in `src/rebar`.\n\n### Change 1 — install the configured provider in the image (the root cause)\n\n`infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp:43`: add `--extra bedrock` to the locked install, giving\n\n```\nuv sync --locked --no-dev --extra agents --extra mcp --extra bedrock\n```\n\nKeep `[agents]` and `[bedrock]` as **separate** extras rather than folding bedrock into\n`[agents]`. The split is a deliberate, documented decision (commit `747e14945593`, ticket\n2932: \"boto3 stays out of the default install\"), and `[agents]` is what every PyPI consumer of\n`nava-rebar` installs — flattening it would push boto3 onto every non-Bedrock adopter to fix a\nproblem that belongs to one deployment. Verified the lock already covers it:\n`uv sync --locked --no-dev --extra agents --extra mcp --extra bedrock --dry-run` exits 0, and\n`pyproject.toml:287-290` records that the former dev/bedrock resolution conflict was removed.\n\n### Change 2 — give the provider a resolvable region (else the fix fails differently)\n\nThe `mcp` service sets no AWS region. The sibling `review-bot` service sets two, with a\nmeasured comment (`docker-compose.yml:143-162`) that in-container\n`boto3.session.Session().region_name` is `None` and that this is independent of IMDS. Bring\n`mcp` to that same, already-proven posture:\n\n- `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` (`mcp` service env): add\n  `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION: \"us-east-1\"` and `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION: \"us-east-1\"`.\n- `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` (`mcp_run_new`): add the same two `-e` flags. This file's own\n  compose comment already requires that the blue-green `docker run` stay at parity with the\n  compose service, and `mcp_run_new` is the path an actual autodeploy takes.\n\nBoth values match the region the instance runs in and the region `rebar.toml:206` pins.\nPurely additive env; the kill switch is deleting the lines.\n\n### Change 3 — RED-first regression guards\n\n`tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py`, following that file's existing image/compose contract\nconvention:\n\n1. A **coupling** guard, not a string match: parse `rebar.toml`'s `[llm] model` and\n   `[llm.model_classes]`, extract every provider qualifier actually configured, map each to\n   the extra that supplies it, and assert `Dockerfile.mcp`'s `uv sync` line installs that\n   extra. This fails today for the right reason, and keeps failing if a future change points\n   the config at a provider the image cannot serve — which is the class of defect, not just\n   this instance.\n2. Region parity: the `mcp` compose service declares both region vars.\n3. Region parity: `mcp_run_new` passes both, so compose and autodeploy cannot drift.\n\n### Explicitly OUT of scope (operator decision required — reported, not changed)\n\n`rebar.toml:209-210` gives the `frontier` and `standard` model classes direct-`anthropic:`\nfallbacks, and `should_fall_back` (`src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py:366-393`) will use them on\nan availability error. The deployed container also receives a real `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` via\n`.env` (`infra/scripts/fetch-secrets.sh:210`), so that arm is live, and it is a non-Bedrock\nprovider reachable by the server. It is **not** removed here because `rebar.toml:181-187`\nrecords an explicit operator decision (2026-08-07, ticket 2876) that the direct-Anthropic arm\nis an approved fallback, and because that table is repo-wide — editing it changes local dev\nand CI, not just this one deployment. Making the deployed server Bedrock-only is a scope\ndecision for the operator, not a bug fix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp` installs the `bedrock` extra alongside `agents` and `mcp`,\n      and the committed lock resolves that extra set (`uv sync --locked ... --dry-run` exits 0).\n- [ ] The `mcp` compose service and `mcp_run_new` in `autodeploy.sh` each set\n      `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION` and `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION` to the instance's region.\n- [ ] A test in `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py` fails when the image's installed extras\n      do not cover every provider configured in `rebar.toml`, and passes after Change 1.\n- [ ] Tests in `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py` assert region parity between the compose\n      `mcp` service and `mcp_run_new`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py`,\n      `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` all pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787868419780257001,"uuid":"e1c483b8-195c-4352-be82-ac11597a5e20"}